Autoimmune Hepatitis (Mild Asymptomatic Transaminitis)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Positive SMA/ANA, mild histological interface hepatitis, asymptomatic clinically

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-004
Reason for Visit: Acute right upper quadrant discomfort following a pathology alert of elevated liver enzymes.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate new-onset right upper quadrant pain in the context of acutely abnormal liver function tests. Rapid assessment was essential to exclude acute cholecystitis, ascending cholangitis, or acute fulminant hepatic failure prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My doctor's clinic sent an automated text about abnormal liver bloods, and now my right side is aching.
History of Present Illness: Patient received an incidental finding of elevated liver enzymes via an automated laboratory SMS this evening. Following this, the patient developed hypervigilance and noted mild right upper quadrant discomfort and slight nausea over the last 3 hours. Denies fever, vomiting, or jaundice.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk, high health anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, no scleral icterus or peripheral stigmata of chronic liver disease.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft and non-distended. Mild, vague tenderness to deep palpation in the right upper quadrant. Murphy's sign is strictly negative. No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly appreciated. History and previous records suggest positive SMA/ANA, mild histological interface hepatitis, asymptomatic clinically, confirming the pain is likely somatic/anxiety-driven rather than an acute capsule stretch.
Environmental Observations: Tidy home, normal temperature, no obvious alcohol containers visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No hepatotoxic medications or recent paracetamol overuse.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Somatic RUQ discomfort in the setting of Autoimmune Hepatitis (Mild Asymptomatic Transaminitis) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, completely negative Murphy's sign, and absence of severe localized pain or guarding.

Acute Fulminant Hepatic Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal mentation, lack of jaundice, and no coagulopathic signs (bruising/bleeding).

Ascending Cholangitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of Charcot's triad (no fever, no jaundice, mild atypical pain).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with subjective acute RUQ discomfort triggered by an automated notification of abnormal liver biochemistry. Triage appropriately escalated the case to rule out acute biliary sepsis or fulminant hepatic failure. Clinical examination confirmed the absence of acute peritoneal signs, jaundice, or fever, determining the presentation to be mild asymptomatic transaminitis complicated by acute health anxiety. This assessment was clinically necessary to eliminate life-threatening biliary pathology that would require immediate surgical intervention or admission, proving it could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the non-critical nature of the blood results. Advised avoidance of alcohol and paracetamol overnight.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink herbal tea and engage in a distraction technique (like reading) for the next hour rather than palpating your abdomen. Ceasing self-examination will reduce local tissue irritation and anxiety, proving that the discomfort is not worsening.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment within 3-5 days to discuss comprehensive liver function tests and autoantibody panels.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you develop severe, unremitting abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, yellowing of your eyes, or a high fever with chills, you must present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately.

